`std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), cmp)` where `cmp` is a lambda taking two `const Student&` and returning `true` when the first must come before the second. Higher score first means `a.score > b.score`; on equal scores compare names with `<`. Never use `>=` or `<=` in a comparator.

```cpp
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](const Student& a, const Student& b) {
    if (a.score != b.score) return a.score > b.score;
    return a.name < b.name;
});
```

`std::accumulate(first, last, init, op)` folds a range. The type of `init` is the type of the running total, so pass `0.0`, not `0`, or the mean is truncated. `op` takes the running total and one element:

```cpp
double total = std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0.0,
    [](double acc, const Student& s) { return acc + s.score; });
return total / static_cast<double>(v.size());   // after checking v.empty()
```
